/// IRET / IRETD (`0xCF`) — interrupt return, re-expressed through
/// `ArmFlow`/`ArmOut` (HLD §4.1, §5 B5).
///
/// RESIDUAL form (the landed `op_grp3`/`op_in_out` shim discipline): the shim
/// does **NOT** `materialize_flags` before the call.  Both the V86 #GP early
/// return AND the main path call `materialize_flags` EARLY and unconditionally
/// (`lib.rs:30013` for the V86 path, `lib.rs:30023` for the main path) — so no
/// reorder is needed for correctness, but to keep the verbatim-move rule and
/// the uniform residual contract this fn owns BOTH `materialize_flags` calls in
/// their EXACT original positions and receives `flags_in` + `lazy_in`
/// un-materialised (zero reorder, maximally faithful).
///
/// An unwatched same-ring successful return restarts the outer decode loop.
/// Watched returns, returns to an outer ring, task/V86 switches, and
/// exceptions remain terminal.
///
/// Flag discipline (verbatim per `lib.rs:30050-30147`):
///   * V86 #GP / IDT/general error paths: `cpu.regs.flags = flags_reg`
///     (materialised) → `out.flags = flags_reg; out.lazy = LazyOp::None`.
///   * `Ok` success (`iret`/`execute_iretd` popped EFLAGS off the stack): the
///     inline arm does `flags_reg = cpu.regs.flags;` (re-read the POPPED flags)
///     then `return Iret` WITHOUT re-writing `cpu.regs.flags`.  This fn sets
///     `out.flags = cpu.regs.flags` (the POPPED value) with `out.lazy ==
///     LazyOp::None`, so the shim's `cpu.regs.flags = out.flags` is the
///     byte-identical redundant write.
///   * `TaskSwitch`/`V86Switch`: inline `executed += 1; cpu.regs.flags =
///     flags_reg;` then return; the pre-advanced `next_eip` is preserved
///     (NOT reset to `ip_val`) exactly as inline.
///
/// `ram` rebinds via `mem.core().ram_base_ptr()` exactly as inline → `out.ram`.
/// `executed` is `+1` on `Ok`/`TaskSwitch`/`V86Switch` and the PRE-increment
/// value on the V86 #GP and IDT/general error paths, exactly as inline.
/// Whether IRET/IRETD must trap to `#GP(0)` before executing, per the V86
/// IOPL rule (Intel SDM vol 3 §20.3.1.1).
///
/// True only in V86 mode with `IOPL < 3`: a V86 monitor has to
/// virtualise the return. Real mode is exempt, and plain protected mode is
/// exempt at ANY IOPL — this guard is V86-specific.
///
/// MDD-BUG-CRUCIBLE-00435 factored this out of `op_iret`. It is a predicate,
/// not just tidiness: the guard's `#GP(0)` is indistinguishable from a
/// downstream `#GP(0)` in the `RunResult`, so end-to-end tests cannot prove
/// the guard stayed silent. They can only prove it fired. Testing the decision
/// directly is what makes "must NOT trap" assertable at all.
fn iret_v86_guard_traps(cpu: &Cpu) -> bool {
    !cpu.is_real_mode() && cpu.is_v86_mode() && cpu.regs.iopl() < 3
}
/// Whether an IF 0->1 transition that sets no interrupt shadow (IRET) ends
/// the run for the outer interrupt poll: always, unless the machine says its
/// board would skip that boundary (`X86Machine::QUIET_POLL_BOUNDARY`).
/// Continuing reaches the outer head, whose budget, wake and latch exits
/// still apply.
#[inline(always)]
fn if_rise_ends_run<M: X86Machine>(mem: &M) -> bool {
    !M::QUIET_POLL_BOUNDARY || mem.poll_boundary_wanted()
}
